Lesson 9 - Probability Distributions
Basic Principle
Real-world data are not random in a completely unstructured way - they follow certain patterns and distributions.

Previous lesson continuation:
Probability ← Distribution ← Inference

What can we do with distributions?
Prediction: What percentage of students will pass the exam?

Risk assessment: How many times will it rain this year?

Quality control: How many out of 10,000 products are defective?

Decision making: Is this medicine reliable?

Two major types of distributions
Discrete: Values that can be counted.

Example: Number of students, number of phones.

Continuous: Values that can be measured.

Example: Height, weight, time.

Most important: Choosing the correct distribution is the basis for making the correct inference.

Random Variable
A variable that assigns a numerical value to the result of an experiment.

Example 1:
When a coin is tossed 10 times, the number of heads is a random variable. The possible number of heads is from 0 to 10, and the data type is discrete.

Example 2:
A person's height can vary. Height is a random variable, and the data type is continuous.

Two types
Discrete random variable

Continuous random variable

Discrete random variable
Values that can be counted.

Example:
Number of children = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...

Probability:

P(X = x)

Continuous random variable
Values that can be measured.

Example:
Height = 170.5, 170.51, 170.512, ...

Important difference
Discrete
P(X = x) has meaning.

Example:

P(X = 3) = 0.2

Continuous
P(X = x) does not have meaning.

Only the following has meaning:

P(a ≤ X ≤ b)

Note: A distribution is the set of all possible values of a random variable and their probabilities.

Four Key Distributions
Normal Distribution
The most common distribution. There are many values near the center and fewer values toward both ends.

Uniform Distribution
All possible outcomes have equal probability.

Poisson Distribution
Represents the number of times an event occurs within a specified time period or area.

Binomial Distribution
In n trials, each trial has two possible outcomes, and it represents the number of successes.

Distribution Selection Decision Process
Question 1: What type of data is it?
Discrete (countable) → Go to Question 2.

Continuous (measurable) → Normal or Uniform.

Question 2: What is the nature of the event?
Binomial or Poisson.

Most important: Choosing the correct distribution is the basis for solving the problem.

Binomial Distribution
What is the Binomial Distribution?
The Binomial Distribution is a probability distribution used to calculate how many times a success occurs in a fixed number of independent trials.

There are 4 conditions for using it:

The number of trials (n) must be fixed in advance.

Each trial has only two possible outcomes.

Success

Failure

The probability of success (p) is the same for every trial.

The trials are independent of each other.

Formula
P(X = x) = C(n,x) × pˣ × (1-p)ⁿ⁻ˣ

When is Binomial used?
✔ Tossing a coin

✔ Number of successful shots

✔ Number of correct answers on an exam

✔ Number of quality products in a factory

✔ Whether a customer buys a product or not

✔ Whether a medical treatment is effective

Poisson Distribution
What is the Poisson Distribution?
The Poisson Distribution is a probability distribution used to calculate how many times an event occurs within a specified time period, interval, or area.

Examples:

How many phone calls arrive in one hour?

How many customers arrive in one day?

How many typing errors are on one page of a book?

How many traffic accidents occur on one kilometer of road?

All of these are examples of the Poisson Distribution.

Conditions for Using Poisson
Events are counted within a specified time period or area.

Events occur independently.

The average number of occurrences (λ) remains constant.

The probability of two events occurring at exactly the same time is very small.

Formula
P(X = x) = e⁻ˡ × λˣ / x!

Or written simply:

P(X = x) = (e⁻ˡ × λˣ) / x!

When is Poisson used?
✔ Number of phone calls received in one hour

✔ Number of customers arriving in one day

✔ Number of typing errors on one page

✔ Number of traffic accidents on a road

✔ Number of emails received in one minute

✔ Number of patients arriving at a hospital

An easy way to recognize it on an exam
If you see the following words in a question, Poisson Distribution is usually used:

Every hour

Every day

Every minute

Average / Mean

How many events occur?

A specified time period or area

Quick review
Binomial = Fixed number of trials (n) + two possible outcomes

Poisson = Fixed time period or area + number of events (λ)

Uniform Distribution
What is Uniform Distribution?
A distribution in which all possible outcomes have equal probability.

In other words, no outcome has a higher or lower probability than another.

Two Types of Uniform Distribution
1. Discrete Uniform Distribution
There are countable, separate values.

Examples: Dice, cards, lottery numbers.

2. Continuous Uniform Distribution
Every value within an interval between two numbers has an equal probability.

Examples: Time, length, weight.

1. Discrete Uniform Distribution Formula
If there are n outcomes with equal probability:

P(X = x) = 1 / n

Or written simply:

P(X = x) = 1 / n

When is Uniform Distribution used?
✔ Rolling a fair die

✔ A fair coin (Head and Tail are each 50%)

✔ Lottery numbers

✔ Selecting a card

✔ Any time within a time interval

✔ Any position within a length or interval

An easy sentence to remember
If all outcomes have equal probability, use Uniform Distribution.

An easy way to recognize it on an exam
If you see the following expressions in a question, Uniform Distribution is usually used:

Equal probability

Fair

Any time

Any position

Every outcome has the same chance

Quick Review
Binomial → Fixed number of trials + two possible outcomes

Poisson → Fixed time period or area + number of events

Uniform → All outcomes have equal probability

Normal → Values near the center are most common, and values toward the ends are less common

Normal Distribution
What is Normal Distribution?
Normal Distribution is the most commonly used distribution in statistics. Values near the center are most common, while values toward the extremes become less common.

Its shape looks like a bell, so it is also called the Bell Curve.

Characteristics of Normal Distribution
The curve is symmetric.

The value at the center is the Mean (μ).

Mean, Median, and Mode are equal.

Values close to the center are most common, while extreme values are least common.

Formula
f(x) = 1 / (σ√(2π)) × e^(-(x-μ)²/(2σ²))

When is Normal Distribution used?
✔ Human height

✔ Weight

✔ Blood pressure

✔ IQ scores

✔ Exam scores

✔ Product measurements in a factory

✔ Many naturally occurring datasets

An easy sentence to remember
If the data are concentrated around the center and become less common toward the extremes, use Normal Distribution.

The easiest way to remember
Binomial → "How many successes?"

Poisson → "How many events occur in a given time?"

Uniform → "All outcomes have equal probability."

Normal → "Most values are in the center, and fewer are at the extremes."

68 – 95 – 99.7 Rule
The most important rule for the Normal Distribution:

Interval	Percentage of data
μ ± 1σ	68%
μ ± 2σ	95%
μ ± 3σ	99.7%
This rule is called the Empirical Rule.

Z-Score
What is Z-Score?
It shows how many standard deviations a value is above or below the mean.

In other words:

Z-Score tells us how far a value is above or below the mean.

Z-Score Formula
Z = (X − μ) / σ

Meaning of Z-Score
Z-Score	Meaning
Z = 0	The value is exactly equal to the mean
Z > 0	The value is above the mean
Z < 0	The value is below the mean
Example 1: Exam Score
For an exam:

Mean score (μ) = 70

Standard deviation (σ) = 5

Ali's score (X) = 80

Step 1: Substitute into the formula
Z = (80 − 70) / 5

Step 2: Calculate
Z = 10 / 5 = 2

Answer
Ali's score is 2 standard deviations above the mean.

Example 2: Height
For students:

Mean height = 170 cm

Standard deviation = 10 cm

Ahmet's height = 160 cm

Formula
Z = (160 − 170) / 10

Calculation
Z = -10 / 10 = -1

Answer
Ahmet's height is 1 standard deviation below the mean.

Example 3: Weight
For a group of people:

Mean weight = 65 kg

Standard deviation = 5 kg

A person's weight = 75 kg

Formula
Z = (75 − 65) / 5

Calculation
Z = 2

Answer
The person's weight is 2 standard deviations above the mean.

How do we understand Z-Score?
If:

Z = 0 → Exactly at the mean.

Z = 1 → 1σ above the mean.

Z = 2 → 2σ above the mean.

Z = -1 → 1σ below the mean.

Z = -2 → 2σ below the mean.

When is Z-Score used?
✔ Comparing exam scores

✔ Evaluating students' heights

✔ Checking weight

✔ Comparing IQ scores

✔ Statistical analysis

Simple Explanation
Imagine:

The average score of students in a class is 70.

If you score 70 → Z = 0

If you score 80 → Z = 2

If you score 65 → Z = -1

Therefore, Z-Score is a measure that shows how far your result is from the mean.

An easy sentence to remember
Z-Score = "How many standard deviations (σ) away from the mean is this value?"

An easy way to recognize it on an exam
If a question gives you the following information, Z-Score is usually used:

Mean (Mean, μ)

Standard deviation (Standard Deviation, σ)

The value of an individual or an object (X)

A question asking, "How much higher or lower is it than the mean?"

Quick Review
Formula:

Z = (X − μ) / σ

If:

Z > 0 → Above the mean

Z = 0 → Equal to the mean

Z < 0 → Below the mean

Main purpose of Z-Score: To measure how far a value is from the mean.

